;; @doc: LLMLL.md §2.4 (Unicode symbol aliases)
;; @expect: check-ok
;; @claim: §2.4 says the Unicode aliases are "accepted everywhere their ASCII equivalents are valid" and that both forms compile to identical AST nodes. Until ALIAS-LOWER-1 the six used here (∧ ∨ ¬ ≥ ≤ ≠) reached `TypeCheck` as raw glyphs, so `check` warned "unknown operator" and `verify` refused; the parser now replaces each with its ASCII name before the node is built, so this program checks clean and its JSON-AST carries `and`, `>=` and so on. It has a second job, the reason it was added under TOOL-ENCODING-1: it is the only fixture whose non-ASCII bytes must survive decoding AND lex into operator tokens, every other one carrying its non-ASCII in a comment or string literal where the lexer skips it.
(module unicode-alias-token
  (export clamp)
  (def clamp [n: int] -> int
    (pre (∧ (≥ n 0) (≤ n 100)))
    (post (¬ (≠ result n)))
    n))
